The first full self-test passed G1–G7 and G9, and the measured reference route costs about 0.7–1.2 million scalar Boolean operations versus 224 XORs for the compact route. G8 correctly caught one collision: complete parity components have too few non-isomorphic support shapes once variable polarity switches are treated as symmetries. I’m fixing the construction itself—not weakening the key—by using a protected full-rank parity basis plus randomly sampled same-distribution parity rows, which preserves inverse generation and creates genuine support-hypergraph diversity.
